From: PoxChat <poxchat@evilnet.org>
Subject: [PATCH] win32: a maximized toplevel keeps the size Windows gave it

Every toplevel layout pass in gdk_win32_toplevel_compute_size() recomputes
the size GTK would like and, when it differs from what the HWND has,
issues a SetWindowPos with that size. For a maximized window that size is
the remembered restored size, and SetWindowPos with an explicit size on a
maximized HWND either shrinks the window in place (still flagged zoomed)
or restores it outright. Any relayout triggers it; opening a popover is
the reliable one, because the popup surface requests a layout of its
parent.

The special case added for GNOME/gtk#7554 only helps when the toplevel
*layout* carries maximized=TRUE, i.e. only after the application itself
called gtk_window_maximize(). A window maximized from the title bar has
no such flag. And that special case pins the minimum size to the work
area, which after the frame is added back is taller than the maximized
window, so app-initiated maximize ends up clipped behind the taskbar; the
pinned minimum also outlives a title-bar restore, so the user's resizes
are undone until the next present.

Decide on the surface *state* instead: while Windows says the window is
maximized (or fullscreen), keep the configured size and never resize it
from a layout pass. Drop the work-area minimum-size hint, which was only
ever a proxy for this.

Tested with GTK 4.20.3 on Windows 11: title-bar maximize + context menu
stays maximized; app maximize no longer overhangs the taskbar; restore
from the title bar followed by a manual resize sticks.

--- a/gdk/win32/gdksurface-win32.c
+++ b/gdk/win32/gdksurface-win32.c
@@ -2780,21 +2780,16 @@
       GdkGeometry geometry;
       GdkSurfaceHints mask;
       GdkToplevelLayout *layout = impl->toplevel_layout;
-      gboolean is_maximized;
       gboolean is_fullscreened;
       GdkRectangle monitor_geometry;
 
+      /* No special case for a maximized layout here: whether the window
+       * is maximized is decided from the surface state in
+       * gdk_win32_toplevel_compute_size(), and pinning the minimum size to
+       * the work area made app-initiated maximize overhang the taskbar. */
       if (monitor &&
-          gdk_toplevel_layout_get_maximized (layout, &is_maximized) &&
-          is_maximized)
-        {
-          geometry.min_width = bounds_width;
-          geometry.min_height = bounds_height;
-          mask = GDK_HINT_MIN_SIZE;
-        }
-      else if (monitor &&
-               gdk_toplevel_layout_get_fullscreen (layout, &is_fullscreened) &&
-               is_fullscreened)
+          gdk_toplevel_layout_get_fullscreen (layout, &is_fullscreened) &&
+          is_fullscreened)
         {
           gdk_monitor_get_geometry (monitor, &monitor_geometry);
           geometry.min_width = monitor_geometry.width;
@@ -3208,21 +3203,46 @@
   int width, height;
   bool size_changed, needs_resize;
 
-  compute_toplevel_size (surface,
-                         TRUE,
-                         impl->next_layout.configured_width,
-                         impl->next_layout.configured_height,
-                         &width, &height);
-
-  needs_resize = impl->force_recompute_size;
-  if (impl->next_layout.configured_width)
-    needs_resize |= width != impl->next_layout.configured_width;
-  else
-    needs_resize |= width != surface->width;
-  if (impl->next_layout.configured_height)
-    needs_resize |= height != impl->next_layout.configured_height;
+  if (surface->state & (GDK_TOPLEVEL_STATE_MAXIMIZED |
+                        GDK_TOPLEVEL_STATE_FULLSCREEN))
+    {
+      /* Windows owns the size of a maximized or fullscreen window. Let
+       * GTK compute its preferred size (that keeps the min-size hints and
+       * the remembered default size current) but keep the size we were
+       * configured with and never SetWindowPos it: an explicit size on a
+       * maximized HWND shrinks it in place or restores it. Any relayout
+       * used to trigger that; opening a popover was the reliable way.
+       * See GNOME/gtk#7554, whose fix only covered layouts that carry the
+       * maximized flag, i.e. app-initiated maximize. */
+      int unused_width, unused_height;
+
+      compute_toplevel_size (surface, TRUE, 0, 0,
+                             &unused_width, &unused_height);
+
+      width = impl->next_layout.configured_width
+              ? impl->next_layout.configured_width : surface->width;
+      height = impl->next_layout.configured_height
+               ? impl->next_layout.configured_height : surface->height;
+      needs_resize = FALSE;
+    }
   else
-    needs_resize |= height != surface->height;
+    {
+      compute_toplevel_size (surface,
+                             TRUE,
+                             impl->next_layout.configured_width,
+                             impl->next_layout.configured_height,
+                             &width, &height);
+
+      needs_resize = impl->force_recompute_size;
+      if (impl->next_layout.configured_width)
+        needs_resize |= width != impl->next_layout.configured_width;
+      else
+        needs_resize |= width != surface->width;
+      if (impl->next_layout.configured_height)
+        needs_resize |= height != impl->next_layout.configured_height;
+      else
+        needs_resize |= height != surface->height;
+    }
 
   size_changed = surface->width != width ||
                  surface->height != height;
